---
import CaseStudy from '../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';

const facts = [
  { k: 'City', v: 'Berlin' },
  { k: 'Year', v: '2014' },
  { k: 'Height', v: '7 stories × 3' },
  { k: 'Homes', v: '~60' },
  { k: 'Residents', v: '~140' },
  { k: 'Architects', v: 'Carpaneto · FAT KOEHL · BAR' },
  { k: 'Tenure', v: 'Co-op / Baugruppe' },
  { k: 'Energy', v: 'Passive house' },
];

const gallery = [
  {
    placeholder: 'Courtyard between buildings — photo needed',
    cap: 'The shared lane between the houses — planting, cargo bikes, option rooms',
    src: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Mitte%20Wilhelmine-Gemberg-Weg.jpg?width=1400',
    credit: 'Fridolin freudenfett · CC BY-SA 4.0',
    creditHref: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mitte_Wilhelmine-Gemberg-Weg.jpg',
  },
  {
    placeholder: 'River path — photo needed',
    cap: 'The bank path — kept public, not privatized',
    src: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Mitte%20Wilhelmine-Gemberg-Weg%20Spreeuferweg.jpg?width=1400',
    credit: 'Fridolin freudenfett · CC BY-SA 4.0',
    creditHref: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mitte_Wilhelmine-Gemberg-Weg_Spreeuferweg.jpg',
  },
  {
    placeholder: 'Cluster apartment interior — photo needed',
    cap: 'A cluster apartment: 600–800 m² shared among many households',
  },
  {
    placeholder: 'Facade — photo needed',
    cap: 'Seven stories over the gravel path between the houses',
    src: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Mitte%20Wilhelmine-Gemberg-Weg-002.jpg?width=1400',
    credit: 'Fridolin freudenfett · CC BY-SA 4.0',
    creditHref: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mitte_Wilhelmine-Gemberg-Weg-002.jpg',
  },
];
---

<CaseStudy
  title="Spreefeld"
  subtitle="Three buildings, one courtyard, a riverbank kept public."
  heroSrc="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Mitte%20Wilhelmine-Gemberg-Weg-001.jpg?width=1400"
  heroPlaceholder="Spreefeld hero — the three buildings from the river (photo needed)"
  heroCredit="Fridolin freudenfett · CC BY-SA 4.0"
  heroCreditHref="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mitte_Wilhelmine-Gemberg-Weg-001.jpg"
  facts={facts}
  gallery={gallery}
  prev={{ href: '/kalkbreite/', label: 'Kalkbreite, Zürich' }}
  next={{ href: '/r50/', label: 'R50, Berlin' }}
>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-[760px] px-6 pt-16 sm:px-12">
    <p class="text-[21px] leading-[1.62]">
      On a stretch of Spree riverbank that speculation should have taken, a cooperative built three
      7-story passive-house buildings — Carpaneto, FAT KOEHL, and BAR Architekten, one each —
      arranged to hold a shared courtyard open to the water. About 60 homes and 140 residents,
      including cluster apartments of 600–800 m² where a dozen people share kitchens and living
      rooms at a scale between flat and floor.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-6 text-[21px] leading-[1.62]">
      The radical move is the ground plane: no housing on it. Kindergarten, workshops, and
      deliberately unprogrammed "option rooms" face the courtyard and the river path, which stays
      open to everyone. Roof terraces connect across bridges. There was no speculative developer
      anywhere in the chain — the residents were the client, and the riverbank shows it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <figure class="mx-auto mt-18 max-w-[1000px] px-6 sm:px-12">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 900 420" class="block h-auto w-full" role="img" aria-label="Site plan: three buildings around a shared courtyard opening to the river">
      <path d="M40 330 C 240 300, 640 300, 860 340 L 860 420 L 40 420 Z" fill="rgba(63,92,69,0.12)" stroke="#3f5c45" stroke-width="2"></path>
      <path d="M40 352 C 240 322, 640 322, 860 362" fill="none" stroke="#3f5c45" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4 7"></path>
      <g fill="rgba(28,25,21,0.08)" stroke="#1c1915" stroke-width="2.5">
        <rect x="150" y="70" width="180" height="120"></rect>
        <rect x="420" y="40" width="140" height="150"></rect>
        <rect x="640" y="80" width="170" height="110"></rect>
      </g>
      <path d="M330 210 C 400 250, 520 250, 640 214" fill="none" stroke="#b44a2f" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 5"></path>
      <g stroke="#3f5c45" stroke-width="2" fill="none">
        <circle cx="420" cy="240" r="16"></circle><circle cx="500" cy="255" r="12"></circle><circle cx="560" cy="232" r="14"></circle>
      </g>
      <g font-family="'Source Sans 3',sans-serif" font-size="13" letter-spacing="2" fill="#1c1915">
        <text x="156" y="58">HOUSE 1 · CARPANETO</text>
        <text x="426" y="28">HOUSE 2 · FAT KOEHL</text>
        <text x="646" y="68">HOUSE 3 · BAR</text>
        <text x="400" y="288" fill="#3f5c45">SHARED COURT, OPEN TO THE RIVER</text>
        <text x="70" y="398" fill="#3f5c45">THE SPREE — BANK PATH STAYS PUBLIC</text>
        <text x="358" y="222" fill="#b44a2f">GROUND FLOOR: NO HOUSING</text>
      </g>
    </svg>
    <figcaption class="mt-3.5 font-sans text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-ink/55">
      Fig. 06 — Spreefeld site logic: buildings hold the court; the court holds the river.
      Illustrative.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <Fragment slot="lessons">
    <li>
      Two or three buildings can make one courtyard — a Berkeley group doesn't need a full-block
      parcel to get the Hof.
    </li>
    <li>
      Keeping the ground floor housing-free is what makes the block a neighbor instead of a
      compound.
    </li>
    <li>
      "Option rooms" — cheap, unfinished, rentable — are how a building stays useful for decades it
      can't predict.
    </li>
    <li>
      Cluster apartments house people the market ignores: singles, elders, groups — at rents whole
      units can't reach.
    </li>
    <li>Passive-house at 7 stories, by a Baugruppe, in 2014: the performance excuse is gone.</li>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="sources">
    Sources: Bau- und Wohngenossenschaft Spreefeld publications and the architects' project pages.
    Figures (~60 homes, ~140 residents) are approximate.
  </Fragment>
</CaseStudy>
